The man page is blunt: a cache belongs to one application class path, one JDK release, and one OS and CPU, and "if any of the above changes, you must recreate the AOT cache". The JVM checks, and in the default auto mode it starts without the cache when a check fails. A changed JAR is the worst case, because on early JDK 25 builds the check was missing. On 25.0.0 to 25.0.3 and on 26.0.0 to 26.0.1, the JVM never compared the application's JARs with the cache (bug JDK-8377932). Rebuild the JAR, keep the old app.aot, and the JVM serves the old classes. This is JDK 25 GA running a JAR rebuilt to print "version TWO": [info][class,load] stale.Hello source: shared objects file version ONE The fix shipped in JDK 27 and was backported to 25.0.4 and 26.0.2. The same run on 25.0.4.1 rejects the cache and runs the new code: [warning][aot] This file is not the one used while building the AOT cache: 'hello.jar', timestamp has changed, size has changed [error ][aot] shared class paths mismatch (hint: enable -Xlog:class+path=info to diagnose the failure) version TWO, rebuilt So require JDK 25.0.4 or later (26.0.2 or later on 26), and rebuild the cache with every JAR, in the same build step, shipped in the same image layer. On a fixed JDK an old cache can't run old code, but it still costs you the whole speed-up. An AOT cache the JVM can't use leaves you with no archive at all. It does not fall back to the default CDS archive. A one-class program loaded 542 classes from the default archive with no flag, and 0 with a stale or missing cache file. A typo in the path makes start-up slower than no flag. A different GC breaks it on JDK 25. A cache trained under G1 gave 0 archived classes under -XX:+UseZGC. Training under ZGC worked only partly: CPU fell from 610 ms to 439 ms, against 504 ms to 81 ms with G1. JDK 26's JEP 516 removes this limit. Until then, train with the GC you run. Module and agent options must match too. --module-path, --add-modules and --enable-native-access should be the same in training and production. Quarkus documents one trap: -XX:StartFlightRecording quietly adds the jdk.jfr module, and the cache no longer matches. The man page also lists "certain JVMTI options" as incompatible. JVMTI is the JVM's tool interface, the one monitoring (APM) agents use to rewrite classes as they load, so this matters as soon as someone attaches an agent. Two flags catch all of this. -Xlog:aot prints Opened AOT cache app.aot. when the cache works and the reason when it doesn't. -XX:AOTMode=on (JDK 24 to 26; required from JDK 27) makes the JVM exit instead of running without the cache. Use that in CI. Keep it out of production: the JDK 27 man page warns that your application "may fail to launch" if something like a cloud provider's monitoring agent adds an incompatible option. Check with the mechanism: you train on JDK 25.0.4 with G1, then deploy the same JAR on 25.0.5 with G1. Does the cache load? (No. The cache records the exact JDK build, and the JVM refuses one made by a different build.) Run new code with an old cache Read StaleCacheDemo. It compiles a one-class program that prints "version ONE", trains a cache on it, rebuilds the JAR to print "version TWO", and starts the new JAR with the old cache. Run it through Gradle. On JDK 21 it uses a dynamic CDS archive, because there is no AOT cache. If you have a JDK 25, run it with that JDK's java directly: java -cp startup/build/classes/java/demo workbench.startup.l02.demo.StaleCacheDemo. Check java -version first and predict which output you'll get. StaleCacheDemo.java package workbench.startup.l02.demo; import javax.tools.JavaCompiler; import javax.tools.ToolProvider; import java.io.OutputStream; import java.nio.file.Files; import java.nio.file.Path; import java.util.ArrayList; import java.util.List; import java.util.jar.JarEntry; import java.util.jar.JarOutputStream; /** * Builds hello.jar printing "version ONE", trains a cache on it, rebuilds the JAR to print * "version TWO", and runs the new